            "summary": "Organization of the gastrointestinal tract, mouth, oral cavity, teeth types, dentition formula, salivary glands, tongue, pharynx, esophagus, and peristalsis.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p>The <strong>Human Digestive System</strong> comprises the <strong>Alimentary Canal</strong> (a continuous muscular tube approximately 8 to 9 meters in length extending from mouth to anus) and associated <strong>Digestive Glands</strong> (salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas). The primary objective of digestion is the mechanical and biochemical breakdown of non-diffusible, complex macromolecules into simpler, diffusible, and absorbable micromolecules.</p>\n<p><strong>Oral Cavity (Buccal Cavity):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Teeth:</strong> Human dentition is characterized as:\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Thecodont:</strong> Each tooth is embedded in a deep socket of the jaw bone (alveolar process).</li>\n    <li><strong>Diphyodont:</strong> Two successive sets of teeth develop during life: primary (deciduous or milk teeth, numbering 20) and permanent (adult teeth, numbering 32).</li>\n    <li><strong>Heterodont:</strong> Four distinct morpho-functional types of teeth exist: Incisors (I, cutting/biting), Canines (C, tearing/piercing), Premolars (PM, grinding/chewing), and Molars (M, crushing/grinding).</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Dental Formula:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Adult Permanent Dentition: $\\frac{2.1.2.3}{2.1.2.3} \\times 2 = 32$ (per upper and lower quadrant: 2 Incisors, 1 Canine, 2 Premolars, 3 Molars).</li>\n    <li>Child Milk Dentition: $\\frac{2.1.0.2}{2.1.0.2} \\times 2 = 20$ (Premolars are completely absent in milk dentition; the 3rd molar or 'wisdom tooth' erupts between ages 17–25).</li>\n    <li><strong>Enamel:</strong> The hard chewing surface of teeth made of calcium hydroxyapatite [$\\text{Ca}_{10}(\\text{PO}_4)_6(\\text{OH})_2$], secreted by <em>ameloblasts</em> (ectodermal origin); it is the hardest substance in the human body.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Tongue:</strong> A freely movable muscular organ attached to the floor of the oral cavity by the <strong>frenulum</strong>. Its upper surface bears small projections called <strong>papillae</strong> (vallate, fungiform, filiform), containing gustatory taste buds detecting sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami tastes.</li>\n<li><strong>Salivary Glands:</strong> Three pairs of major extrinsic salivary glands:\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Parotid Glands:</strong> Largest, located near the ear/cheek; drained by <em>Stensen's duct</em> (viral mumps infection affects parotids).</li>\n    <li><strong>Submandibular / Submaxillary Glands:</strong> Located at the angle of the lower jaw; drained by <em>Wharton's duct</em> (produces ~70% of resting saliva).</li>\n    <li><strong>Sublingual Glands:</strong> Smallest, located beneath the tongue; drained by <em>Ducts of Rivinus</em> / <em>Bartholin's duct</em>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Saliva Composition:</strong> Daily secretion is 1.0–1.5 liters, pH ~6.8 (slightly acidic). Contains 99.5% water, electrolytes ($\\text{Na}^+, \\text{K}^+, \\text{Cl}^-, \\text{HCO}_3^-$), salivary $\\alpha$-amylase (<strong>ptylin</strong>), antibacterial <strong>lysozyme</strong>, and lubricating mucus.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Pharynx & Esophagus:</strong>\nThe oral cavity leads into a common passage for food and air called the <strong>pharynx</strong>. During swallowing (deglutition), a cartilaginous flap called the <strong>epiglottis</strong> closes over the glottis (tracheal entrance) to prevent food from entering the respiratory tract. The <strong>esophagus</strong> is a 25 cm muscular tube penetrating the diaphragm; it produces coordinated involuntary wave-like muscular contractions called <strong>peristalsis</strong> that propel the food bolus downward into the stomach through the <strong>gastroesophageal (cardiac) sphincter</strong>.</p>\n",
