            "title": "Gastric Digestion: Stomach Anatomy, Gastric Juice, HCl, Pepsinogen & Intrinsic Factor",
            "summary": "Stomach regions (cardia, fundus, body, pylorus), gastric glands, parietal/chief/mucous cells, HCl activation of pepsin, Castle's intrinsic factor, and chyme formation.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p>The <strong>Stomach</strong> is a J-shaped muscular reservoir located in the upper left quadrant of the abdominal cavity. It stores ingested food, initiates protein hydrolysis, and converts the food bolus into a semi-liquid acidic mass called <strong>Chyme</strong> through vigorous mechanical churning.</p>\n<p><strong>Stomach Gross Anatomy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiac Region:</strong> Surrounds the superior opening where the esophagus enters via the gastroesophageal sphincter.</li>\n<li><strong>Fundus:</strong> The dome-shaped superior bulge situated above and to the left of the cardiac orifice; typically filled with swallowed air/gas.</li>\n<li><strong>Corpus (Body):</strong> The large central portion where principal chemical digestion and churning occur.</li>\n<li><strong>Pyloric Region:</strong> Tapers into the <em>pyloric antrum</em> and <em>pyloric canal</em>, terminating at the <strong>pyloric sphincter</strong>, which regulates chyme egress into the duodenum.</li>\n<li><strong>Rugae:</strong> Prominent longitudinal mucosal folds that flatten out as the stomach distends, allowing it to hold 1.5 to 2.0 liters of food.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Gastric Mucosa & Glands:</strong>\nThe gastric epithelium invaginates to form tubular <strong>gastric glands</strong> containing specialized cell types:\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Parietal (Oxyntic) Cells:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Secretes <strong>Hydrochloric Acid (HCl):</strong> Generates an intensely acidic environment ($\\text{pH} \\approx 1.5 - 2.0$). HCl kills swallowed pathogens, denatures quaternary protein structures, and activates inactive zymogen <em>pepsinogen</em> into active <em>pepsin</em>.</li>\n    <li>Secretes <strong>Castle's Intrinsic Factor:</strong> A glycoprotein indispensable for the binding and subsequent ileal receptor-mediated endocytosis of <strong>Vitamin B₁₂ (Cyanocobalamin)</strong>. Destruction of parietal cells (autoimmune gastritis) causes <em>Pernicious Anemia</em>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Chief (Peptic or Zymogenic) Cells:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Secretes inactive zymogens: <strong>Pepsinogen</strong> and <strong>Prorennin</strong> (in infants).</li>\n    <li>Acidic HCl cleaves an inhibitory peptide from pepsinogen to generate active <strong>Pepsin</strong> (an endopeptidase cleaving peptide bonds adjacent to aromatic amino acids), which then autocatalytically activates more pepsinogen.</li>\n    <li>Pepsin hydrolyzes complex proteins into soluble <strong>proteoses</strong> and <strong>peptones</strong> (polypeptides).</li>\n    <li><strong>Rennin (Chymosin):</strong> A curdling enzyme in infants that converts milk soluble protein <em>caseinogen</em> into insoluble <em>calcium paracaseinate</em> in the presence of $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$, slowing gastric emptying to enable pepsin digestion. (In adults, milk is curdled directly by HCl and pepsin).</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Mucous Neck Cells & Surface Epithelium:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Secretes alkaline <strong>Mucus</strong> containing bicarbonate ($\\text{HCO}_3^-$) ions, creating a physical and chemical mucosal barrier (pH gradient ~7.0 at cell surface vs ~1.8 in lumen) that prevents auto-digestion of gastric tissue by pepsin and corrosive HCl.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Enteroendocrine (G) Cells:</strong> Located primarily in pyloric glands; secrete the peptide hormone <strong>Gastrin</strong> into the bloodstream upon gastric distension or vagal stimulation, promoting further HCl and pepsinogen secretion.</li>\n</ol>\n</p>\n",
